Z||000005||00lE||08-27-2001||04:39 PM||||Its not a civil matter, and a law suit is not the course of action I would take.  Mail fraud is a felony and even first offenders usually wind up in jail, it pays to know the law:<BR> <A HREF="http://www4.law.cornell.edu/uscode/18/1343.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www4.law.cornell.edu/uscode/18/1343.html</A> <P>You can file a complaint online with the FBI here:<P> <A HREF="https://www.ifccfbi.gov/" TARGET=_blank>https://www.ifccfbi.gov/</A> <P>The prisons are full of idiots that think they can rip off anyone they choose (but find out differently).      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR>All ebay auction transaction's are insured against fraud for up to $200.  You must act within a specifed time, but you could easily recap at least some of your loss:<BR> <A HREF="http://pages.ebay.com/help/basics/f-insurance.html#5" TARGET=_blank>http://pages.ebay.com/help/basics/f-insurance.html#5</A> <P>Just as an example;  I purchased a rather expensive item a few years back over the internet.  It was advertised as being a brand new item, but when the item was recieved it was quite obvious that it was not new.  I immediatly reported the matter to the FBI and they started an investigation within 1 week.  The seller contacted me a few days after that and offered to refund ALL of the money ($1000) I had sent, and specified that I should just keep the item.  It was obvious that he had consulted his attorney to see what his options were and  took his advise.<P>It is also good advice that anyone who sells merchandise on the internet should make every effort to describe exactly what it is they are selling, since misrepresentation is considered to be the same as non-delivery in the eyes of the law.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 08-27-2001).]||||reg||1
